+## 17. 5D2C7 one-boundary reconstruction refusal
+
+No one-boundary graph is reconstructed by 5D2C7. A source-process envelope may contain a valid `universe.committed-native-receipt-chain/1` descriptor, but the only native committed-history reader, `inspect_encounter()`, accepts `PublishedInterval` rows only when `_COMMITTED_RECEIPTS` contains the exact process-local object, Encounter weak reference, and fingerprint. `publish_interval_candidate()` creates that entry only after live publication succeeds. Recreating the entry from decoded bytes would reissue an authority credential, which is expressly excluded.
+
+The descriptor is portable data and does not itself supply an approved constructor/adapter for the complete post-resolution Run/Spot Action graph and Target-owned native history. The existing reconstruction adapter covers unstarted Actions and requires the original clock, empty lifecycle queues, empty fatigue frame history, and no receipt rows. There is no separate quarantine-only native historical projection API. 5D2C7 therefore leaves the initial-only preflight intact and refuses a one-boundary envelope before graph allocation, with an explicit diagnostic identifying the missing projection contract and prohibited process-local receipt issuance. The data-only descriptor remains available for later adapter design; it is not attached as native history or treated as authenticated state.
+
+This increment adds no receipt, Action, Target-history, or historical inspection adapter. `inspect_committed()` continues to reject the reconstructed initial graph and no historical graph can be used for normal inspection. Global `closure_validated`, `portable_schema_defined`, and `restore_verified` remain false. A future bounded step must first specify and validate a detached quarantine-only mapping from `PortableCommittedAction` and `PortableTargetRecord` to fresh native post-boundary Action/Target records, while keeping those records outside `_atomic_interval_history` and preserving the native receipt authentication guard.
