+## 23. 5D2C12C2 inactive historical identity reservation plan
+
+`src/checkpoint_identity_reservations.py` adds a pure plan for the same closed one-boundary/two-Spot sidecar. It first reuses `freeze_imported_spot_practice()` so Choice membership, Action identities, actor/Target ownership, causal effect links and supported-history closure are validated before the identity set is described. The frozen plan lists the historical Choice ID, both Action IDs, root/Event namespace IDs, Target Object IDs and composite causal effect identities. It does not install or reserve any of them in native stores.
+
+Source conventions establish separate scopes: `ChoiceBook._id(kind)` increments one Encounter-local integer and returns `<encounter-id>/<kind>/<counter>`; accepted Choice IDs retain the originating `draft` ID. A historical Choice therefore proves the minimum counter floor given by its positive canonical decimal suffix, and a subsequent draft allocator must start above that floor. Draft and transient validation allocations share the counter, but the supported sidecar omits prior validation/token allocations, so the original exact counter is unknown; the plan reports `source_counter_exact=False` and does not claim an exact resume value. Action IDs are `Identifiable` IDs and are registered in `Universe.event_history` through Choice publication. Thus the origin Event (`0`), Encounter Event and imported Action IDs share the Event key namespace. Target Being IDs belong to the Object registry namespace. Effect identities are composite `(Encounter, boundary, Action, channel, slot)` causal references, not independently allocated IDs.
+
+`validate_future_identity_ids()` checks a proposed new draft ID is in the native Encounter `draft/N` form above the recorded floor and checks proposed Action IDs for duplicates or collisions with imported/root/current Event IDs. It separately checks Choice IDs against current Choice IDs; it does not incorrectly merge the Choice and Event namespaces. Both APIs return detached frozen data or refuse. They do not modify the ChoiceBook counter, native registries, Actions, receipts, histories, RNG, quarantine state or global readiness flags. The plan digest is an edit-detection checksum only, not authentication. No trusted restore binding, allocation, scheduling or continuation is enabled; all checkpoint readiness flags remain false.
+
+## 24. 5D2C12C3 trusted-local preparation, still quarantined
+
+`src/checkpoint_restore_preparation.py` adds a private two-call host policy boundary: host-owned code that has made an out-of-band trusted-local decision calls `_host_authorize_trusted_local_restore(binding)`, then passes the returned process-local opaque capability to `_prepare_trusted_local_restore(binding, capability)`. The capability is tied by object identity to the already privately bound checkpoint and is single-use on successful preparation. No wire field, `trusted=true` value, digest, or imported receipt can create it. This is a trusted-local host policy, not cryptographic origin authentication or a Python security boundary; the repository has no user-facing checkpoint loader and these underscore-prefixed calls are not such a loader.
+
